Break Your App on Purpose (And Fix It)
Video 4 Script (same tone and structure as Video 1)

Today I’m going to break this app on purpose.

And then we’re going to fix it.

No panic.
No drama.
Just calm diagnosis.

This is where you separate yourself from hype YouTube.
Because real builders know how things fail.
And how to respond.

Here is what we are going to do.
We will remove an environment variable.
We will push the change.
We will watch the build fail in Vercel.
We will diagnose the issue.
And we will fix it properly.

The tools are the same as before.
GitHub.
Vercel.
Antigravity.
Supabase.

I assume you already have the project running.
You have a database connected.
You have environment variables set locally and in Vercel.
And everything currently works.

My name is Steve.
I was a professional software tester for over 12 years.
My job was to find where systems break and prove where they remain stable.
So today we’re doing exactly that.
On purpose.

Let’s start.

Right now, the app works.
The database is connected.
Data loads.
Everything feels stable.

Now we remove one environment variable.
For example, the Supabase URL.
We remove it from Vercel.
But we leave the code as it is.

We commit a tiny change.
Or even just trigger a redeploy.

Now we push.

We go to Vercel.
And we watch.

The build starts.
It compiles.
And then it fails.

This is good.

We don’t guess.
We don’t randomly change things.
We read the error.

The error usually tells you exactly what’s wrong.
Missing environment variable.
Undefined value.
Cannot read property of undefined.

Instead of panic, we trace it calmly.

What changed?
We removed a variable.
The app expects it.
The build fails because it cannot initialize the database client.

This is not chaos.
This is logic.

Now we fix it properly.

We go back to Vercel settings.
We restore the missing environment variable.
Exactly as it was.
Correct name.
Correct value.
No typos.

We redeploy.

The build runs again.

This time it passes.

We open the live site.
We refresh.
The data loads again.

Calm.
Controlled.
Resolved.

Here is the lesson.

Most people are afraid of breaking production.
So they never really understand it.

But when you break things intentionally, in a controlled way, you remove fear.
You learn the system.
You learn where the weak points are.
And you learn how to diagnose.

That’s authority.

Not flashy animations.
Not fake revenue screenshots.
Calm competence.

If you can break your own app and fix it without stress, you are no longer dependent on luck.
You are operating a system.

In the next video, we’ll go one layer deeper and look at how to protect yourself from silent failures before they hit production.

See you there.